*/
#ifndef KZIP_H
#define KZIP_H

#include <karchive.h>

#include "kzipfileentry.h" // for source compat

class KZipFileEntry;
/**
 *   @class KZip zip.h KZip
 *
 *   A class for reading / writing zip archives.
 *
 *   You can use it in QIODevice::ReadOnly or in QIODevice::WriteOnly mode, and it
 *   behaves just as expected.
 *   It can also be used in QIODevice::ReadWrite mode, in this case one can
 *   append files to an existing zip archive. When you append new files, which
 *   are not yet in the zip, it works as expected, i.e. the files are appended at the end.
 *   When you append a file, which is already in the file, the reference to the
 *   old file is dropped and the new one is added to the zip - but the
 *   old data from the file itself is not deleted, it is still in the
 *   zipfile. So when you want to have a small and garbage-free zipfile,
 *   just read the contents of the appended zip file and write it to a new one
 *   in QIODevice::WriteOnly mode. This is especially important when you don't want
 *   to leak information of how intermediate versions of files in the zip
 *   were looking.
 *
 *   For more information on the zip fileformat go to
 *   http://www.pkware.com/products/enterprise/white_papers/appnote.html
 * @author Holger Schroeder <holger-kde@holgis.net>
 */
class KARCHIVE_EXPORT KZip : public KArchive
{
    Q_DECLARE_TR_FUNCTIONS(KZip)

public:
    /**
     * Creates an instance that operates on the given filename.
     * using the compression filter associated to given mimetype.
     *
     * @param filename is a local path (e.g. "/home/holger/myfile.zip")
     */
    KZip(const QString &filename);

    /**
     * Creates an instance that operates on the given device.
     * The device can be compressed (KFilterDev) or not (QFile, etc.).
     * @warning Do not assume that giving a QFile here will decompress the file,
     * in case it's compressed!
     * @param dev the device to access
     */
    KZip(QIODevice *dev);

    /**
     * If the zip file is still opened, then it will be
     * closed automatically by the destructor.
     */
    virtual ~KZip();

    /**
     * Describes the contents of the "extra field" for a given file in the Zip archive.
     */
    enum ExtraField {
        NoExtraField = 0,      ///< No extra field
        ModificationTime = 1,  ///< Modification time ("extended timestamp" header)
        DefaultExtraField = 1  // alias of ModificationTime
    };

    /**
     * Call this before writeFile or prepareWriting, to define what the next
     * file to be written should have in its extra field.
     * @param ef the type of "extra field"
     * @see extraField()
     */
    void setExtraField(ExtraField ef);

    /**
     * The current type of "extra field" that will be used for new files.
     * @return the current type of "extra field"
     * @see setExtraField()
     */
    ExtraField extraField() const;

    /**
     * Describes the compression type for a given file in the Zip archive.
     */
    enum Compression {
        NoCompression = 0,     ///< Uncompressed.
        DeflateCompression = 1 ///< Deflate compression method.
    };

    /**
     * Call this before writeFile or prepareWriting, to define whether the next
     * files to be written should be compressed or not.
     * @param c the new compression mode
     * @see compression()
     */
    void setCompression(Compression c);

    /**
     * The current compression mode that will be used for new files.
     * @return the current compression mode
     * @see setCompression()
     */
    Compression compression() const;

    /**
     * Write data to a file that has been created using prepareWriting().
     * @param data a pointer to the data
     * @param size the size of the chunk
     * @return true if successful, false otherwise
     */
    bool writeData(const char *data, qint64 size) override;

protected:
    /// Reimplemented from KArchive
    bool doWriteSymLink(const QString &name, const QString &target,
                        const QString &user, const QString &group,
                        mode_t perm, const QDateTime &atime, const QDateTime &mtime, const QDateTime &ctime) override;
    /// Reimplemented from KArchive
    bool doPrepareWriting(const QString &name, const QString &user,
                          const QString &group, qint64 size, mode_t perm,
                          const QDateTime &atime, const QDateTime &mtime, const QDateTime &creationTime) override;

    /**
     * Write data to a file that has been created using prepareWriting().
     * @param size the size of the file
     * @return true if successful, false otherwise
     */
    bool doFinishWriting(qint64 size) override;

    /**
     * Opens the archive for reading.
     * Parses the directory listing of the archive
     * and creates the KArchiveDirectory/KArchiveFile entries.
     * @param mode the mode of the file
     */
    bool openArchive(QIODevice::OpenMode mode) override;

    /// Closes the archive
    bool closeArchive() override;

    /// Reimplemented from KArchive
    bool doWriteDir(const QString &name, const QString &user, const QString &group, mode_t perm,
                    const QDateTime &atime, const QDateTime &mtime, const QDateTime &ctime) override;

protected:
    void virtual_hook(int id, void *data) override;

private:
    class KZipPrivate;
    KZipPrivate *const d;
};

#endif

message:
I am thinking...: Let me analyze the vulnerability more carefully.

The crash is "Use-of-uninitialized-value" in `parseExtraField` called from `KZip::openArchive`.

Looking at the code in `openArchive` (kzip.cpp lines 522-536):

```cpp
unsigned int extraFieldEnd = dev->pos() + extralen;
pfi.extralen = extralen;
int handledextralen = qMin(extralen, (int)sizeof buffer);  // sizeof buffer = 47

n = dev->read(buffer, handledextralen);
if (!parseExtraField(buffer, handledextralen, true, pfi)) {
```

The buffer is `char buffer[47]` declared on the stack at line 448. It is NOT initialized.

If `extralen` is less than 47, then `handledextralen = extralen`, and `dev->read(buffer, extralen)` reads exactly `extralen` bytes. The remaining bytes of the 47-byte buffer are uninitialized. BUT `parseExtraField` is called with `size = handledextralen = extralen`, so it should only read `extralen` bytes.

Wait, but what if `extralen` is, say, 5? Then `handledextralen = 5`, `dev->read(buffer, 5)` reads 5 bytes. `parseExtraField(buffer, 5, true, pfi)` is called. The while loop checks `size >= 4` → 5 >= 4 → true. It reads magic (2 bytes) and fieldsize (2 bytes), then `size -= 4` → size = 1. If `fieldsize > size` (fieldsize > 1), it breaks. If fieldsize <= 1, it processes the field.

For magic 0x5455 (extended timestamp), `parseExtTimestamp(buffer, fieldsize, true, pfi)` is called. If fieldsize = 1, it reads `flags = *buffer` (1 byte), then `buffer += 1; size -= 1` → size = 0. If `flags & 1` is set, it checks `if (size < 4)` → true (0 < 4), returns false. So `parseExtraField` returns false. That's fine.

But what if fieldsize = 0? Then `parseExtTimestamp(buffer, 0, true, pfi)` is called. It checks `if (size < 1)` → true (0 < 1), returns false. So `parseExtraField` returns false. That's fine.

Hmm, let me think about this differently. The MSAN error is "Use-of-uninitialized-value". This means some variable is being read before it's been written. Let me look at the `ParseFileInfo` struct:

```cpp
struct ParseFileInfo
{
    mode_t perm;
    uint atime;
    uint mtime;
    uint ctime;
    int uid;
    int gid;
    QByteArray guessed_symlink;
    int extralen;
    bool exttimestamp_seen;
    bool newinfounix_seen;

    ParseFileInfo()
        : perm(0100644)
        , uid(-1)
        , gid(-1)
        , extralen(0)
        , exttimestamp_seen(false)
        , newinfounix_seen(false)
    {
        ctime = mtime = atime = time(nullptr);
    }
};
```

The constructor initializes all fields. So `pfi` is properly initialized.

Wait, let me look at the buffer issue again. The buffer is `char buffer[47]` on the stack. It's NOT initialized. When `extralen` is, say, 10, `dev->read(buffer, 10)` reads 10 bytes from the file. The remaining 37 bytes of the buffer contain whatever was on the stack before.

But `parseExtraField` is called with `size = 10`, so it should only read the first 10 bytes. Unless... there's a bug in how the size is tracked.

Actually, wait. Let me re-read `parseExtraField`:

```cpp
while (size >= 4) {
    int magic = (uchar)buffer[0] | (uchar)buffer[1] << 8;
    buffer += 2;
    int fieldsize = (uchar)buffer[0] | (uchar)buffer[1] << 8;
    buffer += 2;
    size -= 4;

    if (fieldsize > size) {
        break;
    }

    switch (magic) {
    case 0x5455:
        if (!parseExtTimestamp(buffer, fieldsize, islocal, pfi)) {
            return false;
        }
        break;
    ...
    }

    buffer += fieldsize;
    size -= fieldsize;
}
```

After processing a field, `buffer += fieldsize` and `size -= fieldsize`. Then the loop continues. If `fieldsize` is small (say 5), and there's another field header in the remaining uninitialized data... but wait, `size` is decremented by `fieldsize`, so it should be accurate.

Actually, I think I found the real bug! Look at line 526:
```cpp
int handledextralen = qMin(extralen, (int)sizeof buffer);
```

`sizeof buffer` is `47` (the size of `char[47]`). But `(int)sizeof buffer` is `47`. If `extralen` is, say, 100, then `handledextralen = 47`. The code reads 47 bytes into the buffer. Then `parseExtraField(buffer, 47, true, pfi)` is called.

But wait, the extra field in the ZIP file might have a structure where the first 4 bytes say "this extra field is 100 bytes long", but only 47 bytes were read. The code handles this with the `if (fieldsize > size)` check.

Hmm, but what if the extra field data is crafted such that:
- First 2 bytes: magic = 0x5455 (extended timestamp)
- Next 2 bytes: fieldsize = 5
- Next 1 byte: flags = 1 (modification time present)
- Next 4 bytes: modification time (but only 4 bytes available after flags, and fieldsize=5 means 5 bytes of content)

Wait, fieldsize=5 means 5 bytes of content after the 4-byte header. flags=1 means 1 byte for flags + 4 bytes for mtime = 5 bytes. That works.

But what if fieldsize=1 and flags=1? Then `parseExtTimestamp` gets size=1, reads flags (1 byte), size becomes 0, then checks `if (size < 4)` → true, returns false. OK.

What if fieldsize=4 and flags=1? Then `parseExtTimestamp` gets size=4, reads flags (1 byte), size becomes 3, checks `if (size < 4)` → true (3 < 4), returns false. OK.

What if fieldsize=5 and flags=1? Then `parseExtTimestamp` gets size=5, reads flags (1 byte), size becomes 4, checks `if (size < 4)` → false (4 >= 4), reads mtime (4 bytes), buffer += 4, size -= 4 → size = 0. Then checks `if (!islocal)` → false (islocal=true). Then checks `if (flags & 2)` → false (flags=1). Then checks `if (flags & 4)` → false. Returns true. OK.

Now, what if fieldsize=9 and flags=1|2=3? Then `parseExtTimestamp` gets size=9, reads flags (1 byte), size=8, reads mtime (4 bytes), buffer+=4, size=4. islocal=true. flags & 2 → true. checks `if (size < 4)` → false (4 >= 4). reads atime (4 bytes), buffer+=4, size=0. flags & 4 → false. Returns true. OK.

What if fieldsize=13 and flags=1|2|4=7? Then reads flags (1), mtime (4), atime (4), ctime (4). Total = 13. Works.

Now, what if fieldsize=6 and flags=1|2=3? Then reads flags (1), mtime (4), size becomes 1. flags & 2 → true. checks `if (size < 4)` → true (1 < 4). Returns true (not false!). 

So `parseExtTimestamp` returns `true` even when there's not enough data for the access time! And then `parseExtraField` does `buffer += fieldsize; size -= fieldsize;` and continues the loop. But the access time was read from uninitialized memory!

Wait, no. Let me re-read:
```cpp
if (flags & 2) {        // contains last access time
    if (size < 4) {
        return true;    // <-- returns true without reading
    }
    pfi.atime = ...;    // reads 4 bytes
    buffer += 4;
    size -= 4;
}
```

If `size < 4`, it returns `true` without reading `pfi.atime`. But `pfi.atime` was already initialized in the constructor, so it's not uninitialized.

Hmm, but what about the buffer itself? The `parseExtTimestamp` function reads from `buffer` which points into the uninitialized stack buffer. If `fieldsize` is larger than the actual data read from the file, `parseExtTimestamp` will read uninitialized stack data.

Wait, but `fieldsize > size` is checked before processing. So if `fieldsize` is 10 and `size` (remaining bytes in the extra field) is 5, the loop breaks. But `size` here is the remaining bytes in the extra field buffer, not the remaining bytes in the file.

Actually, I think the issue is more subtle. Let me trace through a specific example:

1. extralen = 10 (from the ZIP file header)
2. handledextralen = min(10, 47) = 10
3. dev->read(buffer, 10) reads 10 bytes from the file
4. parseExtraField(buffer, 10, true, pfi) is called

Inside parseExtraField:
- size = 10
- Read magic (2 bytes) = 0x5455, fieldsize (2 bytes) = 6
- size = 10 - 4 = 6
- fieldsize (6) <= size (6), so no break
- Call parseExtTimestamp(buffer, 6, true, pfi)
  - size = 6
  - flags = *buffer (1 byte) = 3 (bits 1 and 2 set)
  - buffer += 1, size = 5
  - flags & 1 → true
    - size (5) >= 4 → true
    - Read mtime (4 bytes)
    - buffer += 4, size = 1
  - islocal = true
  - flags & 2 → true
    - size (1) < 4 → true
    - return true (without reading atime!)
  - pfi.atime is NOT updated, but it was initialized in the constructor

So `pfi.atime` is initialized. The MSAN error must be about something else.

Let me think again... The MSAN error says "Use-of-uninitialized-value" in `parseExtraField`. Maybe the issue is that the `buffer` itself contains uninitialized data, and when `parseExtraField` reads from it (e.g., `(uchar)buffer[0]`), it's reading uninitialized stack memory.

But wait, `dev->read(buffer, handledextralen)` reads `handledextralen` bytes into the buffer. If `handledextralen` is less than 47, the remaining bytes are uninitialized. But `parseExtraField` is called with `size = handledextralen`, so it shouldn't read beyond the initialized portion.

Unless... there's an off-by-one or the `fieldsize > size` check is wrong somehow.

Actually, wait. Let me look at this more carefully. The `buffer` is 47 bytes. `sizeof buffer` is 47. But `(int)sizeof buffer` is also 47. So `handledextralen = qMin(extralen, 47)`.

If `extralen = 0`, `handledextralen = 0`, `dev->read(buffer, 0)` reads 0 bytes, `parseExtraField(buffer, 0, ...)` returns immediately. Fine.

If `extralen = 4`, `handledextralen = 4`, `dev->read(buffer, 4)` reads 4 bytes. `parseExtraField(buffer, 4, ...)` reads magic (2 bytes) and fieldsize (2 bytes), size becomes 0. If fieldsize > 0, break. Fine.

If `extralen = 5`, `handledextralen = 5`, `dev->read(buffer, 5)` reads 5 bytes. `parseExtraField(buffer, 5, ...)` reads magic (2 bytes) and fieldsize (2 bytes), size becomes 1. If fieldsize > 1, break. If fieldsize = 1, calls `parseExtTimestamp(buffer, 1, ...)`. In `parseExtTimestamp`, size = 1, reads flags (1 byte), size = 0. If flags & 1, checks size < 4 → true, returns false. So `parseExtraField` returns false. Fine.

Hmm, I'm stuck. Let me look at this from a different angle. Maybe the issue is that the buffer is used AFTER the extra field parsing, and some value from the buffer is used uninitialized.

Actually, let me re-read the code more carefully. After `parseExtraField` returns, the code does:
```cpp
dev->seek(extraFieldEnd);
```

This seeks past the entire extra field. Then it continues processing the file entry. The `pfi` values (mtime, atime, etc.) are used later. But they're all initialized in the constructor.

Wait, I just realized something. Let me look at the `parseExtTimestamp` function again:

```cpp
static bool parseExtTimestamp(const char *buffer, int size, bool islocal,
                                ParseFileInfo &pfi)
{
    if (size < 1) {
        return false;
    }
    int flags = *buffer;
    buffer += 1;
    size -= 1;

    if (flags & 1) {
        if (size < 4) {
            return false;
        }
        pfi.mtime = uint((uchar)buffer[0] | (uchar)buffer[1] << 8
                         | (uchar)buffer[2] << 16 | (uchar)buffer[3] << 24);
        buffer += 4;
        size -= 4;
    }
    if (!islocal) {
        pfi.exttimestamp_seen = true;
        return true;
    }

    if (flags & 2) {
        if (size < 4) {
            return true;    // <-- returns true, doesn't set pfi.atime
        }
        pfi.atime = uint(...);
        buffer += 4;
        size -= 4;
    }

    if (flags & 4) {
        if (size < 4) {
            return true;    // <-- returns true, doesn't set pfi.ctime
        }
        pfi.ctime = uint(...);
        buffer += 4;
    }

    pfi.exttimestamp_seen = true;
    return true;
}
```

When `flags & 2` is set but `size < 4`, the function returns `true` without setting `pfi.atime`. But `pfi.atime` was initialized in the constructor. So this shouldn't cause an MSAN error.

Unless... the issue is that `pfi.atime` is used later in a way that assumes it was set by the extra field parsing. But that's a logic issue, not an MSAN issue.

Let me look at this from yet another angle. Maybe the MSAN error is about the `buffer` itself being read uninitialized. Let me check if `dev->read` can return fewer bytes than requested.

`dev->read(buffer, handledextralen)` returns the number of bytes actually read. If the device returns fewer bytes (e.g., because of EOF), then `n` would be less than `handledextralen`, and the remaining bytes in `buffer` would be uninitialized. But the code doesn't check `n` against `handledextralen`! It just calls `parseExtraField(buffer, handledextralen, true, pfi)` with the full `handledextralen` as the size!

Wait, actually, `QIODevice::read(char *data, qint64 maxSize)` returns the number of bytes read, which could be less than `maxSize`. If the file is truncated, `n` could be less than `handledextralen`. But the code ignores `n` and passes `handledextralen` to `parseExtraField`!

But actually, looking at the code:
```cpp
n = dev->read(buffer, handledextralen);
// no error msg necessary as we deliberately truncate the extra field
if (!parseExtraField(buffer, handledextralen, true, pfi)) {
```

The comment says "no error msg necessary as we deliberately truncate the extra field". So they know they're truncating. But if `dev->read` returns fewer bytes than requested (e.g., because of EOF), then `n < handledextralen`, and the remaining bytes in `buffer` are uninitialized. Then `parseExtraField(buffer, handledextralen, ...)` reads uninitialized data!

This is the bug! If the ZIP file has an extra field with `extralen` bytes, but the file is truncated so that fewer bytes are available, `dev->read` returns fewer bytes, and the rest of `buffer` is uninitialized. Then `parseExtraField` reads from the uninitialized portion of `buffer`.

So the PoC should be a ZIP file where:
1. A local file header has an extra field length > 0
2. The file is truncated so that the extra field data is not fully available

But wait, `dev->read` on a QFile or QBuffer should read as many bytes as available, up to `handledextralen`. If the file is truncated, it would read fewer bytes. But the code ignores the return value `n`.

Actually, let me check: does `QIODevice::read` guarantee to read exactly `maxSize` bytes unless at EOF? For a QBuffer (backed by a QByteArray), yes, it reads up to `maxSize` bytes but may return fewer if there aren't enough bytes left.

So the PoC would be a valid ZIP header with a local file entry that has an extra field, but the file is truncated so the extra field data is incomplete. The `dev->read` would return fewer bytes than `handledextralen`, and `parseExtraField` would read uninitialized stack data.

Let me construct this PoC.

